WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.867
This runnable example demonstrates a business-rule constraint.

00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:08.347
First, run the completed version with a valid appointment time.

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:10.512
PostgreSQL should accept it.

00:00:10.512 --> 00:00:17.037
Then change the ending time so that it is earlier than the starting time and run the example

00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:17.522
again.

00:00:17.522 --> 00:00:24.992
The SQL syntax is still valid, but the requested row now violates the business rule enforced by the

00:00:24.992 --> 00:00:27.167
CHECK constraint. That difference is important.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:33.012
Syntax answers whether the statement is written correctly.

00:00:33.012 --> 00:00:37.372
Constraints answer whether the requested data is allowed.

00:00:37.372 --> 00:00:41.902
Run both versions in the browser and read the error message on the invalid one.

00:00:41.902 --> 00:00:50.332
In pgAdmin, you would see the same idea: a database-level constraint rejects bad data regardless of which

00:00:50.332 --> 00:00:55.942
application submitted it. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:55.942 --> 00:01:02.282
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:02.282 --> 00:01:04.787
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:11.157
In a real setting such as an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines,

00:01:11.157 --> 00:01:18.912
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.237
before discussing syntax.

00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:27.912
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
